#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""barry_launcher_statsd: device stats for Barry Launcher's dashboard on the
AYN Thor's bottom screen.

Serves JSON on 127.0.0.1:PORT for the QML dashboard (dashboard/Dashboard.qml,
a page of the home screen) and its skins, so a skin is plain QML with no
Python of its own:

  GET  /stats     one sample (schema below); sampled on request, at most
                  once per MIN_SAMPLE_S, so nothing runs while nobody asks
  GET  /overlay   {"shown": bool}: OVERLAY, the AYN button's old dashboard
                  flag (nothing sets it now; kept for older skins)
  POST /overlay   {"shown": false} clears it
  GET  /skins     {"skins": [{name, title, author, dir, variants}]}; dir is
                  the current skin's folder under "current"; variants: the
                  skin's color variants [{id, title, swatch: [colors]}]
                  (the first the default), [] for none
  GET  /settings  {"skin": name, "idleSeconds": n, "standbySeconds": n,
                  "heroArt": bool, "bottomSleepSeconds": n,
                  "skinVariants": {skin: id}, "quickLayout": [...] or null,
                  "screensLinked": bool, "volumeLinked": bool}
  POST /settings  any of those keys; skin applies from the next opening,
                  idleSeconds 0 keeps the dashboard in until swiped away;
                  standbySeconds: untouched this long, Barry goes back to
                  the game art (0: never; not over an app or the dashboard);
                  heroArt false: no game art, Barry's apps always shown;
                  bottomSleepSeconds: no touch on the bottom screen (or AYN
                  press) this long and sm8550-thor-backlightd turns its
                  backlight off until the next one (0: never);
                  skinVariants is merged into the saved ones (Barry's
                  keyboards and pads change at once); quickLayout: the
                  quick settings page's widgets in order, [{"id", "span":
                  1-4}] (null: the default, DEFAULT_QUICK_LAYOUT, which
                  GET returns then; ids: QuickSettings.qml's own widgets
                  and "w-<name>" for a widget pack's); screensLinked: the
                  quick settings have one brightness slider for both screens
                  (false: one for each); volumeLinked: one volume for both
                  screens, the top one's (false, the default: one for each)
  GET  /widgets   {"widgets": [{id, name, title, kind, minSpan, span,
                  builtin, ...}]}: every widget the quick settings page can
                  show: Barry's own (kind "barry", BARRY_WIDGETS) and those
                  from packs (kind "pack", WIDGET_DIRS, the user's winning,
                  with author, url, height; see
                  /usr/share/barry_launcher/widgets/README.md; id "w-<name>",
                  as in quickLayout); builtin: shipped, not removable
  GET  /controls  the quick controls:
                  {"fan": {"profile": "balanced", "profiles": [...]} or null,
                   "lighting": {"enabled", "color": "rrggbb", "brightness",
                                "dimmer"}
                               or null,
                   "refresh": {"hz": now, "choice": 60|120|0, "rates": [...]}}
                  (choice 0: none made; games get the panel's highest)
  POST /controls  {"fanProfile": name}, {"lighting": {...any of its keys}}
                  or {"refreshHz": 60|120}; replies as GET
  GET  /quick     the quick settings' system switches (barry_quick):
                  {"wifi": {"enabled"} or null, "bluetooth": {"enabled"}
                   or null, "volume": {"system": 0-100, "muted", "apps":
                   0-100} or null, "capture": {"recording", "seconds",
                   "last": what became of the last recording},
                   "screenOff": {} or null (a device without a Barry button)}
                  (null: the device has none, or its service is down)
  POST /quick     {"wifi": bool}, {"bluetooth": bool}, {"volume":
                  {"system"/"apps": 0-100}}, {"screenshot": true} (the top
                  screen to ~/Pictures/Screenshots), {"record": bool} (the
                  top screen to ~/Videos) or {"screenOff": true} (the bottom
                  screen asleep until a touch); replies as GET, with "saved": the
                  screenshot's file and "error": what went wrong

  POST /volume    {"apps": 0-100}: the bottom screen's apps' volume alone, as
                  /quick's, without reading the rest of it; {"ok": true}

A POST needs a JSON body, a Host of 127.0.0.1 or localhost and no Origin,
so a web page cannot change the fan or the settings.

Fan and lighting go through sm8550-thor-controlsd (root) on CONTROLS. The
refresh rate is Game Mode's: GAMESCOPE_DYNAMIC_REFRESH on Steam's display,
the property Steam's own refresh slider sets, so the two agree; Steam sets it
again when a game with its own refresh setting starts.

/stats, api 1 (fields may be added; skins should ignore unknown ones):
  api, time {text, hour, minute, utcOffset}, fps (null when no game frames
  for 3 s), cpu {ghz, load}, gpu {mhz, maxMhz}, tempC (CPU, the average of
  its sensors), hotC (the hottest sensor), fanPct, powerW
  (negative while discharging), memory {usedGb, totalGb}, battery {percent,
  status}, net {bytesPerSec}

Sources: gamescope's stats pipe (fps= lines; nothing else reads it),
cpufreq, the GPU devfreq node, thermal zones, the pwm fan, the battery and
/proc. Everything is readable without root. Skins live in SKIN_DIRS, first
match wins, so a user skin can replace a built-in one of the same name.
"""
